    <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
Since the cone has an angle of <m>2\pi/3</m> at its vertex,
it has equation
<me>z = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \sqrt{x^2+y^2}.</me>
The top of the cone, the plane with equation <m>z = 3/\sqrt{3}</m>,
intersects the cone in the circle
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>. Thus,
in cartesian coordinates we have
<me>\int_W\, dV = \int_{-3}^{3}\int_{-\sqrt{9-x^2}}^{\sqrt{9-x^2}}
  \int_{\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}^{3/\sqrt{3}}\,1\,dz\,dy\,dx.</me>




<alert>(b)</alert>
In cylindrical coordinates the cone has equation <m>z = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} r</m>, so
the integral becomes
<me>\int_W\,dV = \int_{0}^{2\pi}\int_{0}^{3}\int_{\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} r}^{3/\sqrt{3}}
  r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta.</me>




<alert>(c)</alert>
In spherical coordinates, the cone has equation <m>\phi = \pi/3</m>
and the plane <m>z = 3/\sqrt{3}</m> is <m>\rho\cos\phi = 3/\sqrt{3}</m>.  Thus
we have
<me>\int_W\,dV = \int_{0}^{2\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/3}\int_{0}^{3/\sqrt{3}/\cos\phi}
  \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.</me>



</solution>
